@@s001 LazyList
@@s002 no:not yet computed,ora:forced now,ok:computed
@@s003 <p><code>naturals = from(1)</code> is <b>a single cell</b> with two recipes inside: one for the head, one for the tail. No number has been computed.</p>
@@s004 <p><code>take(3)</code> creates a new cell with <code>cons(h(), t().take(2))</code>. But <code>cons</code> has by-name parameters: <code>h()</code> and <code>t().take(2)</code> <b>are not run</b>. Still zero numbers.</p>
@@s005 <p><code>toList</code> is strict: it wants the values. <code>h()</code> on the first cell of take forces the head of naturals: <b>1</b>.</p>
@@s006 <p>Then <code>t().toList</code>: it forces the tail of take, that is <code>t().take(2)</code>. This forces the tail of naturals, which creates the cell of <code>from(2)</code>, and the second cell of take.</p>
@@s007 <p>Head of the second cell: <b>2</b>.</p>
@@s008 <p>Tail: <code>take(1)</code>. The third cell of naturals and of take is born.</p>
@@s009 <p>Head: <b>3</b>.</p>
@@s010 <p>Tail: <code>t().take(0)</code>. To call <code>take(0)</code> one needs <code>t()</code>: the tail of the third cell is forced and <b>a fourth cell</b> of naturals is born, with the head still to be computed. Then <code>take(0)</code>: the case <code>n &gt; 0</code> does not hold, <code>empty</code>.</p>
@@s011 <p><code>toList</code> on <code>Empty</code> gives <code>Nil</code>: result <b>List(1, 2, 3)</b>. From an infinite list 4 cells have been created and 3 numbers computed. The head of the fourth will never be computed. (Checked by printing every forcing.)</p>
@@s012 due:recipe (not computed),ok:value computed
@@s013 ora:computation run,ok:value remembered,no:computation repeated
@@s014 dato:LazyList,ok:List built,ora:return type
@@s015 spento:only described,due:tail forced (head never computed),ok:head computed
@@s016 ok:true,no:false: stop,ora:still to check
@@s017 no:different values at each read,ok:always the same
@@s018 ora:pass over the list,spento:only described,ok:computed on demand
@@s019 LazyList
@@s020 A <code>LazyList</code> has the same shape as a list, but head and tail are <b>recipes</b> that run only when someone asks for the value. That is why it can be infinite: only the piece you use is computed.
@@s021 Open homework
@@s022 This week's exercises are the assessed hand-in due <b>Tuesday 22 September, 10:00</b>. This page explains the mechanism and shows the code: the hand-in must be written by you ("You must type your hand-in yourself").
@@s023 The type
@@s024 04-lazy-list/Exercises.scala
@@s025 Two shapes, as with <code>List</code>: empty (<code>Empty</code>) or one cell (<code>Cons</code>). The slide says that the two structures are <b>isomorphic</b>: same shape.
@@s026 The difference: the head is not an <code>A</code> but a <code>() => A</code>, a function with no arguments that <b>when you call it</b> gives an A.
@@s027 The tail is a recipe too. Until someone calls it, the rest of the list does not exist.
@@s028 To get the value one calls the recipe: <code>h()</code>. <code>Some</code> wants the value, not the recipe.
@@s029 Head and tail as recipes
@@s030 <code>cons</code>: the convenient constructor
@@s031 The fields of a <code>case</code> cannot be by-name, so <code>Cons</code> uses thunks. To avoid writing them by hand, the companion object offers <code>cons</code>.
@@s032 In the companion object
@@s033 <code>empty</code> is <code>Empty</code> with the right type already written (<code>LazyList[A]</code> instead of <code>Empty.type</code>). It helps the compiler.
@@s034 <b>By-name</b> parameters: whoever writes <code>cons(1 + 1, altro)</code> makes nothing be computed.
@@s035 I put them in <code>lazy val</code>: they will be computed at first use and then <b>remembered</b>. Asking for the head twice computes it only once.
@@s036 I build the <code>Cons</code> with two recipes that read the lazy vals. Note: <code>head</code> inside the recipe is written without <code>()</code>, because it is a lazy val, not a function.
@@s037 <code>LazyList(1, 2, 3)</code> as for lists, but using <code>cons</code>.
@@s038 lazy val: computes once, then remembers
@@s039 What is really computed
@@s040 naturals.take(3).toList
@@s041 <code>toList</code>: when to use <code>::</code> and when <code>cons</code>
@@s042 The type you must return decides
@@s043 I look at the return type: an ordinary <code>List</code>.
@@s044 To build a <code>List</code> I use <code>Nil</code> and <code>::</code>. Value of the head: <code>h()</code>. Rest: the forced tail, <code>t()</code>, turned into a list.
@@s045 If instead I must return a <code>LazyList</code> (as in <code>take</code>), I use <code>cons</code> and <code>empty</code>.
@@s046 Write
@@s047 Builds
