@@s082 :: for List, cons for LazyList, and the right "nothing"
@@s083 <code>take</code> and <code>drop</code>
@@s084 Exercise 3, with the guards
@@s085 There is a cell and I still want at least one more.
@@s086 I keep it, and as the rest I put the <code>take</code> of the rest with one element less. Inside <code>cons</code> nothing is computed now.
@@s087 All the other cases (finite list, or <code>n &lt;= 0</code>): empty list. The case without a guard makes the match exhaustive.
@@s088 <code>drop</code> instead <b>skips</b> the cell: it does not put it in the result, and continues from the tail.
@@s089 When I no longer have to skip, I return <b>the list from here on</b>: <code>this</code>, not <code>empty</code>.
@@s090 A frequent logic error
@@s091 A <code>drop</code> that returns <code>empty</code> on <code>n &lt;= 0</code> compiles, but it is wrong: <code>LazyList(1, 2, 3).drop(0)</code> must give 1, 2, 3, not the empty list. Five tests fail: <code>Ex03.01</code>, <code>Ex03.02</code>, <code>Ex03.05</code>, <code>Ex04.01</code>, <code>Ex10.02</code> (also Fibonacci, because its test uses <code>drop</code>). In your file there was exactly this line, now corrected.
@@s092 
@@s093 naturals.take(1000000000).drop(41).take(10).toList — why is it instantaneous?
@@s094 It creates only the first cell, with recipes inside. Not a billion elements.
@@s095 drop forces 41 tails, one at a time. The heads of those cells are never computed: <code>drop</code> does not look at them.
@@s096 Again only one cell with recipes.
@@s097 toList forces the 10 heads and the tails needed. About fifty cells in all, out of a billion "described" (checked result: 42…51).
@@s098 Exercise 3: the comment required in the file
@@s099 "It terminates because cons is non-strict in its tail: take(1000000000) only builds the first node and keeps the rest as an unevaluated thunk. drop(41) then forces 41 nodes, and take(10).toList forces 10 more. About 51 elements are ever computed; nothing is allocated for the remaining billion."
@@s100 Exercise 2: why does toList fail on an infinite list?
@@s101 "toList is strict: it recurses until it meets Empty, which on an infinite list never happens. The recursion never returns, so it loops forever or overflows the stack."
@@s102 A billion described, fifty computed
@@s103 <code>takeWhile</code>, <code>forAll</code>, <code>exists</code>
@@s104 <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> are not strict: if the left part already decides the result, the right one is not computed. So <code>forAll</code> stops at the first element that fails, <code>exists</code> at the first that succeeds.
@@s105 <b><code>naturals.forAll(_ &lt; 0)</code> terminates?</b> Answer: <b>Yes, at once: false</b>. 1 &lt; 0 is false at the first element: <code>&amp;&amp;</code> does not look at the rest (checked: <code>false</code>).
@@s106 <b>And <code>naturals.forAll(_ &gt;= 0)</code>?</b> Answer: <b>No: it never finds a counterexample and runs out of stack</b>. Every natural is ≥ 0, so it must check to infinity. The text says so: on infinite lists <code>forAll</code> and <code>exists</code> make sense only if you already know the answer.
@@s107 Exercise 4: why is naturals.takeWhile { _ &lt; 1000000000 }.drop(100).take(50).toList instantaneous?
@@s108 "takeWhile builds its result with cons, one node at a time, so it never scans ahead. Only the ~150 elements that drop and take actually request are evaluated. The predicate is never false here, but that does not matter: nobody asks for more."
@@s109 Exercise 5: the two Whys
@@s110 <b>forAll { _ &gt;= 0 }</b>: "forAll stops only when it finds an element that fails the predicate. Every natural is ≥ 0, so it keeps recursing over an infinite list and overflows the stack."
@@s111 <b>forAll and exists on infinite lists</b>: "On an infinite list forAll can only terminate by returning false, and exists only by returning true — so you may use them only when you already know the answer, and then you do not need them. On a finite list they always terminate, because in the worst case they reach the end."
@@s112 && and || stop as soon as they can
@@s113 Two lists of random numbers (slide 14)
@@s114 random1: direct Cons
@@s115 The recipe <code>() => Math.random</code> is run again at <b>each</b> read: <code>random1.take(2).toList</code> gives different numbers each time.
@@s116 random2: with cons
@@s117 <code>cons</code> puts the head in a <code>lazy val</code>: computed once, then always the same.
@@s118 Checked: two calls to <code>random1.take(2).toList</code> give different lists, with <code>random2</code> the same ones. <code>random1</code> is not referentially transparent because <code>Math.random</code> is an effect.
@@s119 Direct Cons against cons
@@s120 Why lazy lists
@@s121 They separate the <b>description</b> of a calculation from its execution: the generator is the same whether 42 numbers or 1000 are needed.
@@s122 Only what is needed is computed, as if everything were in memory.
@@s123 <code>lista.map(f).map(g)</code> on a List goes through the list twice; on a LazyList <b>zero</b> times, until someone asks for the values.
@@s124 They are functional iterators: in Java an <code>Iterator</code> is laziness with <code>next()</code> as the forcing.
@@s125 Description at once, execution on request
